# Testing Rules

## Philosophy

A task is not complete merely because the application starts. It must be tested and verified.

## Test Levels

| Level | Scope | When Introduced |
|-------|-------|-----------------|
| Unit | Individual functions and classes | Milestone 1 |
| Integration | Service + database interactions | Milestone 2 |
| End-to-end | Full request flows across services | Milestone 2+ |
| Container | Docker image behavior | Milestone 3 |
| Kubernetes | Deployment, probes, scaling | Milestone 4 |
| Failure simulation | Network, latency, dependency failures | Milestone 12 |
| Observability | Metrics, logs, traces verification | Milestones 7–10 |
| AI | Prompt quality, tool calling, safety | Milestones 14–17 |

## Expectations

- Write tests alongside implementation, not as an afterthought.
- Tests must be deterministic and repeatable.
- Use appropriate Python testing tools when Milestone 1 begins (e.g., pytest).
- Integration tests live in `tests/integration/`.
- End-to-end tests live in `tests/e2e/`.
- Do not create fake or placeholder tests.

## Verification

- Run appropriate tests after every implementation change.
- Report test results before requesting commit authorization.
- Never claim something works without running verification.
